docs/fidelity.md:91:- `_‑Pu.Run` checks bounds, damps initial scatter before moving, and begins attraction only after scatter speed falls below0.5 (or completion cancels scatter). Acquisition is strictly within100px, independent of class; class cubed affects attraction speed. Field `LifeBubbleCollisionDetection` runs first and collects by alpha contact or the preceding movement's `blnCollected` latch. The sub10px latch therefore grants health on the following field frame. Forced attraction is14px/frame. Collection audio uses a field-owned4→0→-1 counter; there is no browser-clock collection throttle.
docs/fidelity.md:201:- `AssetPack.projectileBounds` supplies field-coordinate Script bounds. Transforms compose down to each declared leaf before union; transparent children and masks contribute without clipping or filter growth. Dynamic beam line/mask widths and endpoints, turret construction/owner-directed rotation, projectile scale and root rotation are included. Morphs use declared start bounds. This follows [Ruffle0.7.1 bounds and hitTestObject](https://github.com/ruffle-rs/ruffle/blob/v0.7.1/core/src/display_object.rs#L1312) and its [morph Script branch](https://github.com/ruffle-rs/ruffle/blob/v0.7.1/core/src/display_object/morph_shape.rs#L114). Edge/corner inclusion and invalid empty bounds were checked against the [rectangle comparator on master](https://github.com/ruffle-rs/ruffle/blob/master/swf/src/types/rectangle.rs#L140); that comparator was not fetched from the pinned tag. An empty authored frame has zero width and no contact geometry, rather than falling back to a mock circle. Mock mode uses its drawn bounds. These are emulator implementation references, not an Adobe Flash runtime comparison.
docs/fidelity.md:249:- User feedback identified that all mock shots used the same tiny `Projectile.radius` circle for contact and generic streak/dot paint. That made homing interception much harder than the source: `_‑QM.TestForCollision:99–138` samples the incoming friendly shot's painted shape, so a radius2 approximation removed most of the original target area. The actual standard1721 shot is12px across; source cannon scale2.5 produces30px, and Massive Cannon1794's child transform produces74.25px. Enemy1717 uses8.5px. Seekers/orbitals retain separate multi-bubble layouts rather than another generic dot.
docs/fidelity.md:260:- `_‑14/_‑c4.TestForCollision:40–68` (pcode243–274) chooses the first enemy clip in the live bullet array with strict distance `<11.5+Script width/2`, without checking enemy-tank count, prior state2, or queued deletion. It writes only state2/moveX/moveY. Ownership, displayed rotation, intrinsic speed, subtype counters and artwork remain intact. Repeated Busters can overwrite the same projectile in a single pass. The previous extra lifetime reset and dead flag filter were removed.
docs/fidelity.md:272:- `BTAGameWorld.Run:489–541` changes gameplay polling in pause/transit without recursively stopping tank children. `_‑Rm:187–194` removes background ENTER_FRAME listeners only. Tank body/weapon poses now use independent `Tank.artAge`, advanced once for the avatar and both attached field populations on every stage step. Cached inactive fields remain detached. `Tank.age` stays with active Run; the old transit-only increment was a rendering workaround and is removed. Painting and part alpha contact consume the same retained stage pose. Exact global stage/ENTER_FRAME phase ordering still needs original-runtime comparison.
docs/fidelity.md:278:- Remaining protection boundaries: controlled Adobe Flash/Ruffle side-by-side recordings, exact constructor/first-frame event interleaving and global random-stream order, simultaneous old hit-clip instances from multiple first-use mounts, and `_‑DO`'s lingering old hit instances. The shipped player sizing path retains intact design parts; unrelated attached children cannot establish a new registration record when centered or tied to those part positions, but arbitrary future attachments would need a general child registry. APassiveForcefield.Run's wrapper-reorder re-enable quirk remains to be modeled. Collision against attached protection/status artwork and full display-tree parity remain open. The subsequent screen-effect checkpoint below replaces ink's former renderer-only lifetime with global effects-engine polling.
docs/fidelity.md:284:- `_‑ar.BulletCallCollisionDetected:15–24` and matching pcode64–116 always queue impact, then test the target's existing ink boolean. `InkMe(120)` (`_‑Zx:134–140`) changes only avatar-design tanks. The screen clip is a separate queue entry gated by forcefield presence, including broken forcefields, and is unaffected by shields. Shipped ink `_‑Jn:24` is enemy=true and can hit the avatar or allies through base collision order. Allies normally never acquire the boolean, so repeated ally hits create overlapping global ink. The former avatar-only `inkView` could not represent that behavior.
docs/fidelity.md:341:- Invisible green muzzle helpers contribute to authored bounds but never procedural paint or alpha contact. Original registration, scale, rotation, shear and depth order are retained. Existing3px mock raster padding keeps the creator's unpadded placement-radius convention; contact reads the rendered alpha mask. Mock protection sizing still uses padded raster extents, unlike native Script bounds, and remains an explicit approximation.
docs/fidelity.md:372:- Each gun stores its latest non-overlapping play episode in stage-art ages. Repeated play while720 runs is a no-op; play after its27-frame loop starts a fresh episode. Coalescing is source-specific:720 has only plain shape718/719 children and no random actions, so completed episodes leave no persistent state or consumed random values. Storage remains constant per mount. The retained playback service materializes the stopped pose before applying play, then catches up through skipped render frames. Native painting and contact use the same retained tree. The mock renderer and alpha contact select the same episode frame.
docs/fidelity.md:393:- Procedural leaf paint uses each source shape's palette, white stroke width and fitted glint placement/orientation. Shared leaf IDs keep the source gold in cached editor/library posters, while hostile live mock mounts receive the existing mild warm distinction. Spike and Massive retain source orange/pink. Painting and alpha contact read the same retained part pose. Attached opacity applies once across animated mounts and fallback bodies. Body artwork and mock protection/status paint remain their existing approximations.
